const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What is LLM as a judge?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'LLM as a judge is an evaluation pattern where one language model grades the output of another. The judge receives the question, the reference answer and the agent answer, then returns a verdict — pass/fail, a score, or a preference between two candidates — along with its reasoning. It replaces human graders in regression testing, so an agent can be evaluated on every pull request instead of once a release.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Why does manual testing of LLM agents not scale?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Two reasons: speed and coverage. Running 500 hand-graded scenarios takes hours, so it cannot run on every pull request. And a hand-written suite only covers the cases someone already imagined — it misses multi-turn context switches, retrieval failures and edge cases you have not thought of yet. The result is reactive testing: users find the bugs, not you.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Is a single LLM judge reliable enough?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'A single judge is cheap and fast — roughly one API call and a couple of hundred milliseconds — but it carries that model’s stylistic bias. It can fail a correct answer purely because the wording differs from the reference. Use a single judge for fast feedback during development, and a panel of several judges whose scores are averaged for decisions that gate a deploy.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Can an agent evaluate its own answers?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'No. Self-judging is the most biased configuration available: the model that produced the answer is the model deciding whether the answer is good. It is a student grading their own exam. Always judge with models other than the one under test.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What is the difference between DeepEval and RAGAS?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'DeepEval is a general LLM evaluation framework with pre-built metrics for hallucination, toxicity, relevance and bias, plus custom LLM-judge metrics — a good fit for agent regression testing in CI. RAGAS specialises in retrieval-augmented generation, scoring faithfulness, answer relevance, context precision and context recall — use it when source attribution and retrieval quality are what you need to measure.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<div class="judge-post">
  <p>
    It's 2 AM on a Tuesday, and your Slack is exploding. A customer just reported a critical bug:
    your AI agent is giving incorrect responses about payment processing. You quickly identify the
    issue — a faulty assumption in the system prompt. You fix it, run a few manual tests, and deploy
    with confidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    By 6 AM, three more bugs appear. Not in payment processing, but in unrelated features: customer
    support queries, knowledge retrieval, report generation. Features you didn't touch. Features you
    thought were working fine.
  </p>

  <div class="incident">
    <span class="tag">The nightmare</span>
    <p>
      Every change to an LLM agent is a potential cascading failure. You cannot see the ripple
      effects of a prompt edit across the rest of the system — and your users are discovering the
      problems you never knew you shipped.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="era">
    <div class="era-head">
      <span class="era-no">PART 01</span>
      <h2>Manual testing doesn't scale</h2>
    </div>

    <p>Your first instinct is reasonable: hire humans to write test scenarios.</p>
    <p>
      You create a test suite of 500 question-and-answer pairs. Each pair has a question and the
      expected answer, written by a domain expert. Every time you change a prompt or modify the
      agent's behaviour, you run all 500 scenarios and mark pass or fail. Simple. Effective.
    </p>
    <p>Then reality sets in.</p>

    <h3>The two scalability killers</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Speed.</strong> Running 500 tests by hand takes hours. Testing every pull request becomes
      a bottleneck, so you stop doing it. You are stuck in reactive mode — users find bugs, you fix them,
      you deploy, more bugs emerge. Proactive testing feels like a luxury you can't afford.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Coverage.</strong> What about the edge cases nobody has thought of yet? Multi-turn conversations
      where the context switches from finance to sports halfway through? RAG answers that need proper
      source attribution? Adding every scenario by hand is an endless treadmill.
    </p>

    <h3>The reactive trap</h3>
    <p>
      You are not discovering problems. Users are — in production. The ideal is the opposite:
      evaluate every change <em>before</em> it reaches users, automatically, at scale, on every pull request.
      This is where most teams give up, because testing LLM agents feels impossible.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="era">
    <div class="era-head">
      <span class="era-no">PART 02</span>
      <h2>The solution: LLM as judge</h2>
    </div>

    <p>
      What if, instead of hiring humans to judge every test, you used an LLM? Here is the insight
      that makes it work: <strong>LLMs are surprisingly good at evaluating other LLMs.</strong> They can
      read a question, compare two answers, and decide which is better — or whether an answer meets a
      standard.
    </p>

    <p>The pattern is simple:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>You make a code change and open a pull request — this triggers the evaluation.</li>
      <li>Your reference dataset (500 question-answer pairs) sits ready as the benchmark.</li>
      <li>Your agent code runs and produces answers.</li>
      <li>
        The judge LLM reads both the reference answer and your agent's answer, then decides whether
        they match — or how close they are.
      </li>
      <li>
        The judge outputs structured results: pass/fail, a score, and reasoning about any mismatch.
      </li>
      <li>You get a report showing exactly which tests failed and why.</li>
    </ol>

