  <section class="era">
    <div class="era-head">
      <span class="era-no">PART 03</span>
      <h2>Three ways to judge — pick your confidence level</h2>
    </div>

    <p>Now you have to decide how confident you need the evaluation to be.</p>

    <h3>Approach 1 — a single judge</h3>
    <p>
      Send the question, the agent answer and the reference answer to one judge LLM. It responds
      <em>pass</em> or <em>fail</em>. Cost: one API call. Latency: roughly 200&nbsp;ms.
    </p>

    <div class="incident">
      <span class="tag">The problem — bias</span>
      <p>
        LLMs have preferences. One model prefers a formal tone, another conversational. One is
        strict about exact wording, another lenient. Consider:
      </p>
      <p class="pair">
        <span class="lab">Reference</span> The capital of France is Paris.<br />
        <span class="lab">Agent</span> Paris is the capital of France.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both are correct. But your judge might reply: <em
          >"I prefer the reference format. The agent answer is inconsistent. Fail."</em
        > That is bias — a valid answer marked as failing because the judge has stylistic preferences.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      <strong>When to use it:</strong> quick, cheap feedback during development. Not for decisions that
      gate a release.
    </p>

    <h3>Approach 2 — a panel of judges</h3>
    <p>
      Send the same question and answers to several judges at once, then average their scores. Each
      model has different biases, so the biases largely cancel out. An outlier — one judge at 0.65
      while the rest sit around 0.90 — is isolated instead of sinking the result.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <svg
        viewBox="0 0 720 210"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A panel of four judges scoring the same answer: 0.92, 0.88, 0.89 and 0.90, averaged to 0.897. One outlier at 0.65 barely moves the consensus."
      >
        <text
          x="8"
          y="24"
          font-size="11"
          font-family="ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace"
          letter-spacing="1"
          fill="var(--accent)"
