    <h3>Approach 3 — self-judging (don't)</h3>
    <p>
      Someone will suggest letting the agent evaluate itself. Don't. This is maximally biased — the
      model is motivated to mark its own answer correct. You are asking a student to grade their own
      exam.
    </p>

    <div class="tablebox">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Approach</th>
            <th scope="col">Cost</th>
            <th scope="col">Bias risk</th>
            <th scope="col">Use it for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Single judge</td>
            <td class="num">1×</td>
            <td>High</td>
            <td>Fast local feedback while iterating on prompts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Judge panel</td>
            <td class="num">4×</td>
            <td>Low</td>
            <td>Release gates, regression suites, anything that blocks a deploy</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Self-judge</td>
            <td class="num">1×</td>
            <td>Maximum</td>
            <td>Nothing</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="era">
    <div class="era-head">
      <span class="era-no">PART 04</span>
      <h2>What should you actually evaluate?</h2>
    </div>

    <p>Not every aspect of agent behaviour matters equally. Prioritise by use case.</p>

    <h3>1. Single-turn Q&amp;A — the baseline</h3>
    <p>
      Simple question, simple answer. Is it correct? <em
        >"What is the Python keyword for looping?"</em
      > →
      <code>for</code>. This is your foundation. If the agent fails here, nothing else matters.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Multi-turn conversations — the real challenge</h3>
    <p>
      A conversation with context, where the user asks several questions and sometimes switches
      topic. This is where most agents fail. Can yours remember it was talking about Bitcoin, or
      will it start explaining the Chiefs' blockchain strategy? The judge evaluates <strong
        >context coherence</strong
      >: did the agent hold its thread across the topic switch?
    </p>
    <p>
      Multi-turn evaluation is harder to build — you need conversation histories, not just Q&amp;A
      pairs — but it catches the real-world failures single-turn tests never see.
    </p>

    <h3>3. RAG evaluation — grounding in reality</h3>
    <p>
      If your agent uses retrieval-augmented generation, its answers should be grounded in the
      retrieved sources. The judge checks three things: is the answer supported by the document, are
      the citations valid, and did the agent hallucinate?
    </p>

    <div class="metric">
      <span class="tag">Grounded</span>
      <p class="pair">
        <span class="lab">Document</span> The Earth orbits the Sun in 365.25 days.<br />
        <span class="lab">Agent</span> The Earth orbits the Sun in 365.25 days, as stated in Document_42.<br
        />
        <span class="lab">Judge</span> Answer is supported by the source. Pass.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="incident">
      <span class="tag">Hallucinated</span>
      <p class="pair">
        <span class="lab">Agent</span> The Earth orbits Mars, a fact not covered in the documents.<br
        />
        <span class="lab">Judge</span> Unsupported claim. Fail.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="era">
    <div class="era-head">
      <span class="era-no">PART 05</span>
      <h2>What judges output, and how to use it</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="tablebox">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Output</th>
            <th scope="col">Looks like</th>
            <th scope="col">Answers the question</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Binary pass/fail</strong></td>
            <td class="num">487 passed · 13 failed</td>
            <td>"Did this PR break anything?"</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Numerical score</strong></td>
            <td class="num">87/100 · relevance 0.84</td>
            <td>"Is the agent getting better?" — graph it over time</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Pairwise comparison</strong></td>
            <td>Model B wins, because…</td>
            <td>"Which of these two prompts is better?"</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Reference-based</strong></td>
            <td>Cited ✓ · valid ✓ · faithful ✓</td>
            <td>"Can I trust this RAG system to cite honestly?"</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Binary output is the simplest and the right default for regression testing. Scores are more
      nuanced and worth tracking as a time series — a quality graph that slopes downward over three
      sprints tells you something no single build ever will. Pairwise comparison is what you want
      for A/B testing prompts. Reference-based checks belong to RAG systems specifically.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="era">
    <div class="era-head">
      <span class="era-no">PART 06</span>
      <h2>Tools: DeepEval and RAGAS</h2>
    </div>

    <p>You don't need to build judge logic from scratch. Two frameworks dominate this space.</p>

    <div class="tablebox">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Framework</th>
            <th scope="col">What it gives you</th>
            <th scope="col">Best for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>DeepEval</strong></td>
            <td>
              Hallucination detection, toxicity scoring, relevance checking, bias detection, and
              custom metrics backed by an LLM judge
            </td>
            <td>General agent evaluation, regression testing in CI, scoring answers</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>RAGAS</strong></td>
            <td>
              Faithfulness (is the answer grounded?), answer relevance, context precision (were the
              retrieved documents relevant?), context recall (did retrieval miss anything?)
            </td>
            <td>RAG pipelines where source attribution matters</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="era">
    <div class="era-head">
      <span class="era-no">FAQ</span>
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    </div>

    <h3>What is LLM as a judge?</h3>
    <p>
      One language model grading the output of another. The judge receives the question, the
      reference answer and the agent answer, then returns a verdict — pass/fail, a score, or a
      preference between two candidates — with its reasoning. It replaces human graders in
      regression testing, so the agent can be evaluated on every pull request instead of once a
      release.
    </p>

    <h3>Is a single judge reliable enough?</h3>
    <p>
      For fast feedback while you iterate, yes. For anything that gates a deploy, no — one model's
      stylistic preference can fail a correct answer. Use a panel and average the scores.
    </p>

    <h3>Can an agent evaluate its own answers?</h3>
    <p>
      No. Self-judging is the most biased configuration available. Always judge with models other
      than the one under test.
    </p>

    <h3>How big should the reference dataset be?</h3>
    <p>
      Big enough that a regression in any feature you care about has at least a handful of scenarios
      pointing at it. Start with the traffic you actually see — real production questions with
      expert answers beat synthetic ones — and grow the set every time a user finds something your
      suite missed.
    </p>

    <h3>What is the difference between DeepEval and RAGAS?</h3>
    <p>
      DeepEval is the general evaluation framework — hallucination, toxicity, relevance, bias and
      custom judge metrics. RAGAS is specialised for retrieval: faithfulness, answer relevance,
      context precision and context recall. Reach for RAGAS when retrieval quality is the thing
      under test.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="era">
    <div class="era-head">
      <span class="era-no">CLOSING</span>
      <h2>2 AM incident, or 2 AM code review</h2>
    </div>

    <p>
      Traditional testing caught bugs after users found them. LLM judges let you catch them before
      they reach production.
    </p>
    <p>
      This isn't perfect. LLMs make mistakes. Your reference dataset will be incomplete. Consensus
      scoring costs real money. But the alternative — hoping your changes don't break things — is
      worse.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pattern is emerging: LLMs evaluating LLMs. It is not just a test framework, it is a
      different way to think about quality. Every time you deploy an agent, you are not only
      shipping code — you are shipping a system that can evaluate itself and defend against its own
      degradation.
    </p>
    <p>
      That 2 AM production incident? With judges catching regressions in CI, it becomes a 2 AM fix
      in code review instead. Not zero incidents. But better. Much better.
    </p>
  </section>

